Read this as edge-row geometry, not isolated prime numerology:
  multiplying the gap by a new odd prime removes fewer residue classes,
  so the whole row gets a Hardy-Littlewood singular-series boost.

================================================================================================================
Goldbach/twin duality in (sum, gap) coordinates
================================================================================================================
For same-parity endpoints, the coordinate change is invertible:
  endpoints = ((sum-gap)/2, (sum+gap)/2)
  twin primes: fixed gap=2
  Goldbach:    fixed even sum=N

      N  Goldbach pairs       gap range    best carry pair   carry
    100               6           6..94           (11, 89)       0
    500              13         42..474          (13, 487)       4
   1000              28         18..994           (3, 997)       5
   5000              76       234..4986        (673, 4327)       0
  10000             127       162..9882       (3209, 6791)       4
  50000             450       66..49986        (61, 49939)       0
 100000             810      246..99978     (24971, 75029)       0

  Fixed-sum columns usually contain many possible gaps; fixed-gap rows
  usually contain many possible sums.  The same edge can be read both ways.

================================================================================================================
A small prime-pair surface sample
================================================================================================================
Prime pair edges with endpoints <= 400 and gap <= 40: 539
Gap row counts:
  2:21 4:21 6:42 8:19 10:27 12:36 14:25 16:21 18:35 20:25 22:18 24:37 26:22 28:20 30:48 32:18 34:21 36:39 38:18 40:26
Most common sum residues mod 30:
  0:68 24:60 6:60 18:53 12:49 10:34 20:31 22:28 28:26 14:23
Most common midpoint residues mod 30:
  0:38 27:33 3:32 15:30 24:29 18:28 12:27 21:25 6:24 9:24

The residue data is a reminder that the pair-cell carries local obstruction
data.  A prime-pair edge is an arithmetic connection, not just two prime nodes.

================================================================================================================
Tournament edge board as the finite unsieved analogue
================================================================================================================
n=14: total pair-cells C(n,2)=91
  range rows: 1:13 2:12 3:11 4:10 5:9 6:8 7:7 8:6 9:5 10:4 11:3 12:2 13:1
  entropy by range is triangular before any orientation; Tournament Analysis then adds one sign bit per cell.
n=18: total pair-cells C(n,2)=153
  range rows: 1:17 2:16 3:15 4:14 5:13 6:12 7:11 8:10 9:9 10:8 11:7 12:6 13:5 14:4 15:3 16:2 17:1
  entropy by range is triangular before any orientation; Tournament Analysis then adds one sign bit per cell.

Analogy:
  tournament board: all pair-cells exist, then orientation signs decorate them.
  prime-pair board: all integer pair-cells exist, then the sieve deletes most cells.
  LRC board: all runner pair-cells exist, then time changes their metric labels.

================================================================================================================
SC blowup: pair cells become twin lanes
================================================================================================================
For each old tournament pair {u,v}, SC blowup creates four edge-cells:
  lane:  u0-v0, u1-v1 follow T
  cross: u0-v1, u1-v0 follow T^op

This is why 'twin' is the right word inside the repo too:
  the old pair is not discarded; it is doubled into lane/cross structure.
  Score variation is erased at the vertex level, but remembered in pair labels.
  That mirrors twin primes: the node property prime is less informative than
  the repeated connection type gap=2 surviving the sieve.

================================================================================================================
Zeckendorf carry debt on prime-pair edges
================================================================================================================
Twin prime edge audits:
        pair    sum            raw support             target  score
      (3, 5)      8                 (3, 4)               (5,)      4
      (5, 7)     12                 (2, 4)          (1, 3, 5)      7
    (11, 13)     24              (3, 5, 6)             (3, 7)      4
    (41, 43)     84        (1, 2, 4, 5, 8)          (5, 7, 9)     10
  (137, 139)    276       (1, 3, 6, 8, 10)      (1, 5, 8, 12)     11
  (347, 349)    696   (1, 3, 4, 7, 10, 12)   (2, 5, 7, 9, 14)     18
  (881, 883)   1764   (1, 3, 4, 8, 12, 14)     (2, 7, 11, 16)     19

Best carry Goldbach edge in selected columns:
    sum               pair            raw support             target  score
    100           (11, 89)             (3, 5, 10)         (3, 5, 10)      0
    500          (13, 487)         (6, 7, 10, 13)        (8, 10, 13)      4
   1000           (3, 997)          (2, 3, 5, 15)            (6, 15)      5
   5000        (673, 4327)  (2, 5, 9, 11, 14, 18) (2, 5, 9, 11, 14, 18)      0
  10000       (3209, 6791) (2, 4, 6, 7, 14, 17, 19) (2, 4, 8, 14, 17, 19)      4
  50000        (61, 49939)  (1, 4, 9, 15, 17, 23) (1, 4, 9, 15, 17, 23)      0

This gives a repo-native metric for prime edges:
  how much local carry work is needed to collapse endpoint supports to the
  support of the sum.  It is pair data, not endpoint data.

================================================================================================================
New questions suggested by the pair-first lens
================================================================================================================
1. Can Tournament Analysis store every pair-cell as (range, midpoint, sign, flip-impact) and only later project to vertex scores?
2. Can prime-pair work store every edge as (sum, gap, residue singular factor, carry debt) and compare fixed-sum vs fixed-gap fibers?
3. Is the right Goldbach/twin bridge a TDA of the prime-pair surface, with holes created by local residue deletions and filled by singular-series boosts?
4. For LRC, do pressure DAGs become clearer if their nodes are runner-pairs or endpoint-runner pairs rather than runners alone?
5. Can SC blowup be used as the finite tournament model of an arithmetic twin operation: duplicate carriers, keep connection memory, flatten node scores?
